{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Elohim blessed the seventh day.</b> Rashi, of blessed memory, explained: He blessed it with the manna and sanctified it with the manna — thus far his words. But the glory of the Rabbi rests in its place; it is not fitting that he would allude to the future. Rather, the correct interpretation is that He blessed it with the renewal that was in it on that day, which is the blessing of light, for the day and the night of the seventh day are equal in light, and this is the blessing and the addition of good. Therefore you will not find in the seventh day any mention of evening and morning as in the other days, for its entire time is morning — there is in it no evening at all. And it says, ״ויקדש אותו״ — 'and He sanctified it,' that is, to set it apart from the other days, for the one set apart from the affairs of the world is called holy; and so Israel, who are set apart from the nations, were called ״גוי קדוש״ — 'a holy nation' — and it is written (Leviticus 19): ״קדושים תהיו״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: the seventh day is the seventh sephirah, called the World of Souls, for there is the power and root of the soul. And it is the source of the blessings. The soul on the weekdays is like a guest without his home, and on the Sabbath day like a guest in his home. Therefore it says ״ויברך״ — 'and He blessed' — for He caused to flow upon it from the source of blessing; and likewise ״ויקדש אותו״ — 'and He sanctified it' — for it draws from the holiness. And understand this." "<b>Which Elohim created to do.</b> The work that was fit to be done on the Sabbath, He doubled and did on the sixth. And the Ramban, of blessed memory, explained: ״שבת מכל מלאכתו אשר ברא״ — 'He rested from all His work which He created' — that is, from nothingness, ״לעשות״ — 'to do' — from it all the deeds mentioned in the six days. And so it says that He rested both from creation and from making: from the creation that He created on the first day, from the making that He made on the rest of the days — thus far his words." "<b>And it is possible</b> to explain that the reason the account of Creation was sealed with ״ברא אלהים״ is that so too it began with it: ״בראשית ברא אלהים״. And the intent is to suspend the entire work upon one Maker, and to indicate that He, blessed be He, is the One who begins and completes, at the beginning and at the end. And the reason He added ״לעשות״ is that since He, blessed be He, created until now — and further brought forth and produced all existents from nothing — He decreed upon the existents to do their actions from something, henceforth and onward." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah, the addition of ״לעשות״ — I have already alluded to it above. And so David, upon him be peace, alluded (Psalms 92:13): ״מזמור שיר ליום השבת״ — 'A psalm, a song for the Sabbath day' — and afterward he mentioned (ibid.): ״צדיק כתמר יפרח כארז בלבנון ישגה״. And the discerning will understand." "<b>And in the Midrash of Rabbi Eliezer.</b> They expounded that heaven and earth are destined to pass away and be renewed. What is written of them? (Isaiah 34:4): ״ונגולו כספר השמים״ — 'and the heavens shall be rolled together like a scroll' — like a person who reads from a Torah scroll and rolls it shut, then returns and opens it and reads and rolls it again; so the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to roll the heavens like a scroll, as it says: ״ונגולו כספר השמים״, and ״והארץ כבגד תבלה״ — 'and the earth shall wax old as a garment' — like a person who takes off his tallit and folds it and puts it on again; so the Holy One, blessed be He, is destined to fold the earth like a garment, as it says: ״והארץ כבגד תבלה״. Rabbi Eliezer says: all the host of heaven is destined to wither and be renewed, as it says (ibid.): ״וכל צבאם יבול כנבול עלה מגפן״ — 'and all their host shall wither as a leaf withers from the vine.' Just as this vine and fig tree — their leaves wither and stand like a dry tree, then they sprout again and blossom and put forth fresh new leaves — so all the host of heaven is destined to wither like a vine and fig tree, and they are renewed before Him, to make known that there is an El who consumes yet is not consumed. End quote." "<b>And know</b> and understand that these seven days of Genesis explain to us what has passed and hint to us about the future. Behold, they correspond to six thousand years and one desolate; and our Rabbis of blessed memory expounded: one day of the Holy One, blessed be He, is a thousand years, as it says (Psalms 90:4): ״כי אלף שנים בעיניך כיום אתמול״. And it is clear that each and every day of these six days of Genesis hints at things destined to occur in the world in that millennium corresponding to that day. On the first day the supernal light was created; corresponding to it is the first millennium, in which Adam the first man was created, who was the light of the world. And from the fact that on the first day you find 'light' (אור) five times and 'darkness' (חשך) three times, you can understand that in the first millennium the signs of light and good and tranquility prevailed over the signs of darkness and evil. The second day — corresponding to it is the second millennium, in which the Flood occurred; therefore ״כי טוב״ was not said of it, for it is unfit to mention good in the destruction of the world. And since the created beings were not fully actualized and established until the great deed of HaShem came, which is the Flood, therefore ״ויהי כן״ was not mentioned but only after ״ויעש״. And for this reason our Rabbis of blessed memory said: one does not begin on the second — that is, on the second day — because it is not fitting to begin a task on the day the Holy One, blessed be He, destroyed His world with the Flood. And if you think that they forbade beginning tasks on the second day because the services of the firmament, which are harmful and damaging — this is not so, for the night of the second day is ruled by Tzedek (Jupiter) and the day of the second day is ruled by the Levanah (the Moon), and both are stars of light and rectitude, fitting to begin every task with them; how could the Sages have forbidden the beginning of tasks on a day ruled by Tzedek and Levanah? The third day corresponds to the third millennium, and in it ״כי טוב״ is mentioned twice: once for the second day, for the annulment of the decree of the Flood, and the second for the Torah, which is called 'good,' destined to be given in the third millennium, which is 2448 (אלפים תמ״ח). And just as the Holy One, blessed be He, gave the earth permission to bring forth its great ones, and they did not appear but stood at the surface of the ground until Adam came — he being the essence of the world — so in the third millennium they were fruitful and multiplied, as it is written (Exodus 1:7): ״ובני ישראל פרו וישרצו וירבו וגו׳״, and the holiness and excellence of Israel were not apparent to the people of the world until the Torah came, which is the essence and the endurance of the world. The fourth day corresponds to the fourth millennium, in which sovereignty and tranquility and great excellence belonged to Israel. And at that time the wisdom of Israel was broader than the sea; this is what is written: ״יהי מאורות״ — ״את שני המאורות הגדולים״ — ״את המאור הגדול לממשלת היום״ — this is the Written Torah; ״ואת המאור הקטן לממשלת הלילה״ — this is the Oral Torah; ״ואת הכוכבים״ — these are the baraitot and toseftot and mekhiiltot and hagadot that are joined with the Oral Torah, for indeed the stars are counted with the lesser luminary. The fifth day corresponds to the fifth millennium, in which we dwelt in exile among the idolaters, who are likened to creeping living souls; and all of it, from beginning to end, was for us an exile; therefore ״ויהי כן״ is not said of the fifth day, because our exile is not forever, but our end is for redemption — and when? On the sixth day, when the Messiah comes, of whom it is intimated in the phrase ״נעשה אדם״. The sixth day corresponds to the sixth millennium, in which we are today fifty-one years. And just as at the beginning of the sixth day it says ״תוצא הארץ נפש חיה למינה בהמה ורמש וחיתו ארץ״, so in the sixth millennium at its beginning we have in it exile, and we are subjugated under the hand of the nations. And behold, the beasts and wild animals were created before the shining of the sun, which is the time of their dominion, as it is written (Psalms 104:22): ״תזרח השמש יאספון וגו׳״; and when the sun shone, man was created, which is the time of his dominion, as written (Psalms ibid.): ״יצא אדם לפעלו״. And just as man came at the morning of the sixth day — this is what is said: ״נעשה אדם״ — so the Messiah will come at the morning of the sixth millennium, after a tenth of the millennium, according to the measure of sunrise for a day. And this is Messiah son of David, of whom it is written (Psalms 89:37): ״וכסאו כשמש נגדי״; and he is the one made in the image of Elohim, as it is written (Daniel 7:13): ״וארו עם ענני שמיא כבר אנש אתי הוה״. And this will be at the time of 118 years after the fifth millennium, to complete the vision of Daniel, who said (Daniel 12): ״ימים אלף מאתים ותשעים״, and this is the calculation precisely. It is known that 172 years after the destruction of the Temple, four thousand years of creation were completed. Add 172 to 118 and it comes to 290, and that is the precise reckoning. And what it says after it (ibid.): ״אשרי המחכה ויגיע לימים אלף שלש מאות שלשים וחמשה״ — they are 45 years after 118; then all the wars and all the zealous conflicts shall pass. Therefore it depended on אשרי (happy), for between the redemption of the outcast and the redemption of the scattered there are 45 years. So too we find in the Second Temple: 18 years between the decree and the building of the Temple; and from the redemption from Egypt until they entered the land, 40 years. These 45 years after 118 bring you to a reckoning of 163, and that is the long calculation — and it is impossible to delay further in any manner beyond 163 and onward, for the days of redemption for Israel are 437 years, and this is intimated (Isaiah 9:6): ״לםרב״ה המשרה״, whose numerical value is 437, for the mem is closed. And this is what is written (Song of Songs 7:1): ״כמחולת המחנים״ — hinting that the days of dance and sovereignty for Israel in the future redemption shall be like the days of standing in tranquility and goodness formerly: they are 410 years for the First Temple and 420 years for the Second Temple and 7 years that they were engaged in building the First Temple, which were also days of joy and dance — behold, 437. Subtract from the sixth millennium 437, which are the days of our future redemption, and 163 years after the fifth millennium remain, in which all Israel shall be redeemed and all the wars shall pass and peace shall be without end. And if you add 172 — the completion of four thousand — to 163, it comes precisely to one thousand three hundred and thirty-five days (years). And behold, this is the explanation of ״עד עדן עדנין ופלג עדן״, if you look into it. And it appears that it is without a vav, even though we find in precise books ״עדן ועדנין״ with a vav, because עדן standing adjacent to עדנין is not part of the count; but when it says ״עד עדן עדנין ופלג עדן״, that is the count. And the first עדן shall be from when Israel left Egypt until the First Temple was built, which is 480 years, as it says (1 Kings 6:1): ״ויהי בשמונים שנה וארבע מאות שנה לצאת בני ישראל מארץ מצרים ויבן הבית לה׳״; and the second עדן is 410 years that the Temple stood in its building — and the two עדנין are 890 (תת״ן). Add to them their halves, which are 445 (תמ״ה), and it comes precisely to one thousand three hundred and thirty-five — and this is the same calculation itself (Daniel 12:7): ״כי למועד מועדים וחצי״, that is, for the time of the two מועדים; and when you combine them and know their number, divide it in half — this is what it says ״וחצי״, and it did not say ״וחצי המועד״. And when you add to the two מועדים half of both, you will still find it all equal, and the meaning of ״ופלג עדן״ refers to the first עדן that is not part of the count, not to the עדן of the two עדנין. And now I shall rouse you and hint to you regarding this matter: you will find the precise reckoning of this calculation in the word באדונ\"י in the full spelling of its letters — for אל\"ף דל\"ת וא\"ו נו\"ן יו\"ד; remove the initial letters of the Name and the remaining letters will yield you, in numerical value together with the addition of the bet, one thousand three hundred and thirty-five. For at that time the lordship of HaShem, blessed be He, shall be vindicated, and there shall be rest and tranquility. And likewise with the matter of the name מנוח in full spelling, as it says (Judges 13:8): ״ויעתר מנוח אל ה׳ ויאמר בי אדוני״ — and what is intimated in the full spelling of the name מנוח is to hint that in the time of rest (מנוחה) His lordship shall be proclaimed." "<b>And behold,</b> this is the secret of the matter: that He sealed all the troubles that passed in all the days with ״טוב מאד״, to indicate that as soon as the redemption comes at the morning of the sixth millennium, all the troubles shall be forgotten, as it is written (Isaiah 65:16): ״כי נשכחו הצרות הראשונות וכי נסתרו מעיני״. And because of this it says ״יום הששי״ — with the definite article, the hey — by way of praise and exaltation of the day, to make known that the redemption shall be open and publicized throughout the world. The seventh day corresponds to the seventh millennium, which is entirely Sabbath and rest for the life of the worlds; and it is after the days of the Messiah and the resurrection of the dead, and those who arise in the resurrection of the dead shall merit it and delight in body and soul with delight that has no end." "<b>And in my opinion,</b> to this Shammai and Hillel were hinting when they said: 'מחד שביך לשבתיך' — 'from the first day of your week, prepare for your Sabbath.' For this saying embraces matters of both body and soul. For just as it is fitting for the eyes of the body to look out throughout all six days toward the day of Shabbat, to set aside a fine portion and good share for the needs of Shabbat, so too the eyes of the intellect look out toward the passing of six thousand years, that they may merit the excellence of the Shabbat, which is the seventh millennium. If so, these Sages were not boasting of matters of the body alone, but rather of the sustenance of the soul, which is eternal and enduring. And understand this."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:10:10.911242768Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}